# Chains and archives

Download this `.livemd` file, open it in [Livebook](https://livebook.dev), and choose
**Open → Import from file**, then **Run all**. Use Elixir 1.18 or later and OTP 27 or later
with access to public Hex. No secrets or external services are needed.

Build a deployment evidence chain, anchor its boundaries, authenticate an archive-key
transition, and verify an immutable archive object against host-held expectations.

Chain, anchor, and transition facts carry `trust: :not_evaluated`. An anchored export also
carries `authorization: :not_evaluated`. The host owns completeness, trusted historical keys,
commitment semantics, archival storage, and audit policy. This notebook keeps bytes in memory
and starts no services or files; it does not contact an object store.

[Open this 1.5.0 release notebook](https://livebook.dev/run?url=https%3A%2F%2Fgithub.com%2Fbaselabs%2Fbounded_authority_protocol%2Fblob%2Fbaselabs_bounded_authority_protocol-v1.5.0%2Fdocs%2Fnotebooks%2Fchains-and-archives.livemd).
The launch opens this journey at its immutable release tag.

## Setup

Install the exact published package. Fixed evaluation times keep the results reproducible;
only safe semantic values are persisted, never keys, signatures, tokens, or nonces.

```elixir
Mix.install([{:bounded_authority_protocol, "== 1.5.0"}])
```

<!-- livebook:{"output":true} -->

```
:ok
```

## Encode the consumption chain

Each row commits to a host record and links to its predecessor. Sequence one begins at the
all-zero hash; later rows carry the hash of the previous canonical row.

```elixir
alias BoundedAuthorityProtocol.V1
alias BoundedAuthorityProtocol.V1.{ConsumptionEntry, ChainInput, ExpectedChain}

chain_id = "urn:example:chain:deployments"
genesis = <<0::256>>

# The host's commitment for each accepted invocation, here a digest of a record it keeps:
commitments =
  for record <- ["deploy web 1.4.2", "deploy api 2.0.1", "rollback web 1.4.1"],
      do: :crypto.hash(:sha256, record)

{rows, head} =
  commitments
  |> Enum.with_index(1)
  |> Enum.map_reduce(genesis, fn {commitment, sequence}, previous ->
    entry = %ConsumptionEntry{
      chain_id: chain_id,
      sequence: sequence,
      previous_hash: previous,
      commitment: commitment
    }

    {:ok, encoded} = V1.encode_consumption_entry(entry, %{})
    {encoded.bytes, encoded.hash}
  end)

length(rows)
```

<!-- livebook:{"output":true} -->

```
3
```

## Verify the expected chain boundary

Use the predecessor and head held by the host, not boundaries chosen from the input. A
self-consistent shortened chain alone cannot establish completeness.

```elixir
expected_chain = %ExpectedChain{
  chain_id: chain_id,
  first_sequence: 1,
  last_sequence: 3,
  row_count: 3,
  previous_hash: genesis,
  last_hash: head,
  bounds: %{}
}

{:ok, chain_facts} = V1.check_chain(%ChainInput{rows: rows}, expected_chain)

Map.take(chain_facts, [:row_count, :verification, :trust])
```

<!-- livebook:{"output":true} -->

```
%{row_count: 3, verification: :boundary_consistent, trust: :not_evaluated}
```

## Refuse changed rows and boundaries

Missing, reordered, truncated, or noncanonical rows fail the host-held boundary. The producer
also refuses a first row with a nonzero predecessor.

```elixir
[first, second, third] = rows

{:error, :invalid} = V1.check_chain(%ChainInput{rows: [first, third]}, expected_chain)
{:error, :invalid} = V1.check_chain(%ChainInput{rows: [first, third, second]}, expected_chain)

{:error, :invalid} =
  V1.check_chain(%ChainInput{rows: [first, second]}, %{
    expected_chain
    | last_sequence: 2,
      row_count: 2
  })

{:error, :invalid} =
  V1.check_chain(
    %ChainInput{
      rows: [String.replace(first, "\"sequence\":1", "\"sequence\":1 "), second, third]
    },
    expected_chain
  )

{:error, :invalid} =
  V1.encode_consumption_entry(
    %ConsumptionEntry{
      chain_id: chain_id,
      sequence: 1,
      previous_hash: <<1::256>>,
      commitment: hd(commitments)
    },
    %{}
  )

[:dropped, :reordered, :truncated, :noncanonical, :wrong_predecessor]
```

<!-- livebook:{"output":true} -->

```
[:dropped, :reordered, :truncated, :noncanonical, :wrong_predecessor]
```

## Prepare historical archive keys

Generate two archive keys and give each a host-owned validity window. Their fingerprints
use the raw RFC 7638 thumbprint construction.

```elixir
alias BoundedAuthorityProtocol.V1.{BoundaryAnchor, ExpectedAnchor, HistoricalPublicKey}

{key_a_public, key_a_private} = :crypto.generate_key(:eddsa, :ed25519)
{key_b_public, key_b_private} = :crypto.generate_key(:eddsa, :ed25519)
{:ok, key_a_fingerprint} = V1.Jwk.public_key_thumbprint_raw(key_a_public, %{})
{:ok, key_b_fingerprint} = V1.Jwk.public_key_thumbprint_raw(key_b_public, %{})

key_a = %HistoricalPublicKey{
  key_id: "archive-2026-a",
  public_key: key_a_public,
  valid_from: 1_799_990_000,
  valid_before: 1_800_010_000
}

key_b = %HistoricalPublicKey{
  key_id: "archive-2026-b",
  public_key: key_b_public,
  valid_from: 1_800_005_000,
  valid_before: :unbounded
}

sign = fn input, private_key ->
  signature = :crypto.sign(:eddsa, :none, input.message, [private_key, :ed25519])
  {:ok, compact} = V1.assemble_compact(input, signature)
  compact
end

:archive_keys_ready
```

<!-- livebook:{"output":true} -->

```
:archive_keys_ready
```

## Sign the start anchor

Key A signs the chain head before any rows exist: sequence zero and the all-zero hash.
The signed timestamp will be checked against key A’s validity window.

```elixir
{:ok, start_input} =
  V1.boundary_anchor_signing_input(
    %BoundaryAnchor{
      anchor_id: "urn:example:anchor:deployments:start",
      anchored_at: 1_800_000_000,
      chain_id: chain_id,
      sequence: 0,
      chain_hash: genesis,
      key_id: "archive-2026-a",
      public_key: key_a_public
    },
    %{}
  )

start_anchor = sign.(start_input, key_a_private)

:start_anchor_signed
```

<!-- livebook:{"output":true} -->

```
:start_anchor_signed
```

## Verify the start anchor

Supply the anchor identity, time, sequence, head, and key fingerprint from host records.
Verification authenticates only this expected statement under the supplied historical key.

```elixir
expected_start = %ExpectedAnchor{
  anchor_id: "urn:example:anchor:deployments:start",
  anchored_at: 1_800_000_000,
  chain_id: chain_id,
  sequence: 0,
  chain_hash: genesis,
  key_id: "archive-2026-a",
  key_fingerprint: key_a_fingerprint,
  bounds: %{}
}

{:ok, anchor_facts} = V1.verify_historical_anchor(start_anchor, key_a, expected_start)

Map.take(anchor_facts, [:sequence, :verification, :trust])
```

<!-- livebook:{"output":true} -->

```
%{sequence: 0, verification: :signature_and_window, trust: :not_evaluated}
```

## Refuse the wrong anchor key or key window

A valid anchor signature is insufficient outside the historical key window or under a
different archive key.

```elixir
{:error, :invalid} =
  V1.verify_historical_anchor(start_anchor, %{key_a | valid_from: 1_800_000_001}, expected_start)

{:error, :invalid} = V1.verify_historical_anchor(start_anchor, key_b, expected_start)

[:outside_key_window, :other_key]
```

<!-- livebook:{"output":true} -->

```
[:outside_key_window, :other_key]
```

## Sign the archive-key transition

Key A signs the identity and fingerprint of key B. The effective time is inside both key
windows; no remote key discovery occurs.

```elixir
alias BoundedAuthorityProtocol.V1.{KeyTransition, ExpectedKeyTransition}

{:ok, transition_input} =
  V1.key_transition_signing_input(
    %KeyTransition{
      transition_id: "urn:example:transition:deployments:1",
      chain_id: chain_id,
      effective_at: 1_800_006_000,
      current_key_id: "archive-2026-a",
      current_public_key: key_a_public,
      next_key_id: "archive-2026-b",
      next_public_key: key_b_public
    },
    %{}
  )

transition = sign.(transition_input, key_a_private)

:transition_signed
```

<!-- livebook:{"output":true} -->

```
:transition_signed
```

## Verify the transition

The verifier requires the exact current and successor keys and expected transition context.
It authenticates a historical link, leaving the root of trust to the host.

```elixir
expected_transition = %ExpectedKeyTransition{
  transition_id: "urn:example:transition:deployments:1",
  chain_id: chain_id,
  effective_at: 1_800_006_000,
  current_key_id: "archive-2026-a",
  current_key_fingerprint: key_a_fingerprint,
  next_key_id: "archive-2026-b",
  next_key_fingerprint: key_b_fingerprint,
  bounds: %{}
}

{:ok, transition_facts} =
  V1.verify_key_transition(transition, key_a, key_b, expected_transition)

Map.take(transition_facts, [:verification, :trust])
```

<!-- livebook:{"output":true} -->

```
%{verification: :authenticated_transition, trust: :not_evaluated}
```

## Refuse reversed transition keys

The next key cannot stand in for the current signer. Swapping the supplied key history must
fail even though both keys are otherwise valid.

```elixir
{:error, :invalid} = V1.verify_key_transition(transition, key_b, key_a, expected_transition)
```

<!-- livebook:{"output":true} -->

```
{:error, :invalid}
```

## Sign the end anchor

Key B signs sequence three and the chain head after the transition. The host separately
records the expected end boundary.

```elixir
alias BoundedAuthorityProtocol.V1.{AnchoredExportInput, ExpectedExport}

{:ok, end_input} =
  V1.boundary_anchor_signing_input(
    %BoundaryAnchor{
      anchor_id: "urn:example:anchor:deployments:end",
      anchored_at: 1_800_007_000,
      chain_id: chain_id,
      sequence: 3,
      chain_hash: head,
      key_id: "archive-2026-b",
      public_key: key_b_public
    },
    %{}
  )

end_anchor = sign.(end_input, key_b_private)

expected_end = %ExpectedAnchor{
  anchor_id: "urn:example:anchor:deployments:end",
  anchored_at: 1_800_007_000,
  chain_id: chain_id,
  sequence: 3,
  chain_hash: head,
  key_id: "archive-2026-b",
  key_fingerprint: key_b_fingerprint,
  bounds: %{}
}

:end_anchor_signed
```

<!-- livebook:{"output":true} -->

```
:end_anchor_signed
```

## Frame the anchored export

Encode the rows, both anchors, and the transition against the expected boundaries. The
returned chunks form one object with an exact byte count and SHA-256 digest.

```elixir
{:ok, export} =
  V1.encode_anchored_export(
    %AnchoredExportInput{
      rows: rows,
      start_anchor: start_anchor,
      transitions: [transition],
      end_anchor: end_anchor
    },
    %ExpectedExport{
      chain: expected_chain,
      start_anchor: expected_start,
      transitions: [expected_transition],
      end_anchor: expected_end,
      bounds: %{}
    }
  )

export_bytes = IO.iodata_length(export.chunks)
^export_bytes = export.byte_count

%{chunks: length(export.chunks), digest_bytes: byte_size(export.digest)}
```

<!-- livebook:{"output":true} -->

```
%{chunks: 8, digest_bytes: 32}
```

## Verify the immutable archive object

The object version and digest are out-of-band host records. Verify the complete bytes and
ordered key history against them, including exact end of object.

```elixir
alias BoundedAuthorityProtocol.V1.{ArchivedObject, ExpectedAnchoredExport, HistoricalKeyChain}

archived = %ArchivedObject{chunks: export.chunks, version: "object-version-7"}
key_history = %HistoricalKeyChain{keys: [key_a, key_b]}

expected_archive = %ExpectedAnchoredExport{
  chain: expected_chain,
  start_anchor: expected_start,
  transitions: [expected_transition],
  end_anchor: expected_end,
  digest: export.digest,
  object_version: "object-version-7",
  bounds: %{}
}

{:ok, archive_facts} = V1.verify_anchored_export(archived, key_history, expected_archive)

Map.take(archive_facts, [
  :row_count,
  :transition_count,
  :verification,
  :trust,
  :authorization
])
```

<!-- livebook:{"output":true} -->

```
%{
  row_count: 3,
  transition_count: 1,
  verification: :anchored_export,
  trust: :not_evaluated,
  authorization: :not_evaluated
}
```

## Verify identical bytes with different chunking

Chunk boundaries are transport details. Recombining the same bytes does not change the
archive identity or its verification result.

```elixir
all_bytes = IO.iodata_to_binary(export.chunks)

{:ok, rechunked_facts} =
  V1.verify_anchored_export(%{archived | chunks: [all_bytes]}, key_history, expected_archive)

Map.take(rechunked_facts, [:row_count, :verification, :authorization])
```

<!-- livebook:{"output":true} -->

```
%{row_count: 3, verification: :anchored_export, authorization: :not_evaluated}
```

## Refuse archive identity or history changes

Appending a byte, changing the object version or digest, omitting key history, or dropping
the expected transition fails closed. None can be repaired by trusting the archive’s claims.

```elixir
{:error, :invalid} =
  V1.verify_anchored_export(
    %{archived | chunks: export.chunks ++ [<<0>>]},
    key_history,
    expected_archive
  )

{:error, :invalid} =
  V1.verify_anchored_export(
    %{archived | version: "object-version-8"},
    key_history,
    expected_archive
  )

{:error, :invalid} =
  V1.verify_anchored_export(archived, key_history, %{expected_archive | digest: <<0::256>>})

{:error, :invalid} =
  V1.verify_anchored_export(archived, %HistoricalKeyChain{keys: [key_a]}, expected_archive)

{:error, :invalid} =
  V1.encode_anchored_export(
    %AnchoredExportInput{
      rows: rows,
      start_anchor: start_anchor,
      transitions: [],
      end_anchor: end_anchor
    },
    %ExpectedExport{
      chain: expected_chain,
      start_anchor: expected_start,
      transitions: [expected_transition],
      end_anchor: expected_end,
      bounds: %{}
    }
  )

[:trailing_byte, :object_version, :digest, :key_history, :missing_transition]
```

<!-- livebook:{"output":true} -->

```
[:trailing_byte, :object_version, :digest, :key_history, :missing_transition]
```

The package checks evidence but does not keep it. The host appends records atomically,
chooses when to anchor, stores archives, and records each digest and object version. See
[evidence](../guides/evidence.md) and [operations](../guides/operations.md) for those boundaries.
